WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.920
Heart failure is a
supply problem with different mechanical

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:03.297
routes.

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:06.775
We use the same scholarly ventricular anatomy for both

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:07.385
comparisons.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.360
The moving volume markers
are schematic, not measured chamber

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:11.780
volumes.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.778
In systolic failure,
impaired contraction reduces ejection.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.733
More blood remains after the beat, and stroke volume

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:19.056
falls.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:23.133
The chapter connects reduced
output with activation of compensatory

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:23.783
systems.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:28.410
In diastolic failure, relaxation
and compliance are abnormal.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.318
Filling is restricted even
when ejection fraction is preserved.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:35.564
A preserved fraction does
not guarantee that enough blood

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.660
enters and leaves the ventricle.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.590
The slides distinguish pulmonary
congestion in left failure from

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.874
systemic venous congestion in right failure.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:47.545
Orthopnea and pulmonary edema
belong to the pulmonary side;

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:53.128
jugular venous distension and
peripheral edema identify systemic congestion.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:57.452
Chapter thirty two, slides
eighty nine, ninety and ninety

00:00:57.452 --> 00:01:00.244
two through ninety five, support the comparison.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:05.495
Specific diagnostic cutoffs and
treatment selection require additional textbook

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:05.980
evidence.
